To fix the CVE-2026-59313 vulnerability in spring-webmvc, you need to override the version pulled in by spring-boot-starter-web.
Because spring-webmvc is a core part of the Spring Framework, the safest way to upgrade it without causing version conflicts (like NoSuchMethodError between different Spring modules) is to upgrade the entire Spring Framework version via your <properties> block.
Here are the two ways to fix it in your pom.xml:
Option 1: The Best Practice (Update all Spring Framework modules)
Add the spring-framework.version property to your <properties> section. This ensures spring-webmvc, spring-core, spring-context, etc., all stay on the same secure version.
    <properties>
        <java.version>21</java.version>
        <tomcat.version>11.0.25</tomcat.version>
        <maven.compiler.source>21</maven.compiler.source>
        <maven.compiler.target>21</maven.compiler.target>
        <jackson-bom.version>3.2.2</jackson-bom.version>
        <jackson-2-bom.version>2.22.2</jackson-2-bom.version>
        
        <!-- Add this line to force Spring Framework to 7.0.9 -->
        <spring-framework.version>7.0.9</spring-framework.version>
    </properties>

Option 2: The Direct Override (Target exactly this package)
If you strictly want to override only spring-webmvc and leave the rest of the framework alone, add it to your existing <dependencyManagement> block.
    <dependencyManagement>
        <dependencies>
            <!-- Add this override at the top of your dependencies list -->
            <dependency>
                <groupId>org.springframework</groupId>
                <artifactId>spring-webmvc</artifactId>
                <version>7.0.9</version>
            </dependency>

            <!-- 2. Netty BOM (RESTORED TO 4.2.16.Final...) -->
            <dependency>
                <groupId>io.netty</groupId>
                <artifactId>netty-bom</artifactId>
                <version>4.2.17.Final</version>
                <type>pom</type>
                <scope>import</scope>
            </dependency>
            
            <!-- ... rest of your dependencyManagement ... -->

Recommendation: Go with Option 1. It is much safer to keep the Spring Framework suite on the exact same version than to mix and match a newer spring-webmvc with an older spring-core. Once added, run mvn clean install to pull in the new secure version.
